timeshift-ssh (25.12.4+ssh34) resolute; urgency=medium

  * Fix `timeshift --list` against an SSH repository: nothing created
    /run/timeshift/<pid>, so ssh could not bind its control socket and the
    listing reported the location as unavailable
  * engines.Repository now carries the write path, so the daemon no longer
    asserts its way back to the concrete engine type
  * jobs.pause and jobs.resume actually suspend the work: SIGSTOP to the
    whole process group, not just a flag
  * snapshots.browse mounts a remote snapshot read-only with Timeshift's own
    key and hands the path to the client, which opens it as the desktop user

 -- makeafide <willsmit4433@gmail.com>  Mon, 31 Aug 2026 22:22:00 -0400

timeshift-ssh (25.12.4+ssh33) resolute; urgency=medium

  * The Go core: timeshiftd owns the config, the job queue and the schedule,
    and serves them over /run/timeshift/daemon.sock, so a snapshot started by
    apt-snapshot-guard can be watched from the GUI while it runs
  * Repository writes are serialised by a flock both cores take (RepoLock /
    internal/replock), replacing AppLock's refusal of a second process. Two
    windows may now be open; two backups may not run at once
  * timeshiftd clears up after runs that were killed: mounts left under
    /run/timeshift/<pid>, orphaned ssh control sockets, empty run directories
  * The scheduler is the daemon, not cron. The /etc/cron.d drop-ins are swept
    on upgrade and at daemon startup
  * timeshift --restore, --create, --delete, --estimate, --check and --watch
    run through the daemon; --list prefers it and falls back when absent
  * New timeshift system group: members get the read-only IPC subset, so
    watching a running backup needs no pkexec

